{"at":"2026-10-19T21:03:23.710Z","event":"prompt","agent":"remakecraft:spec-writer","by":"main","sha256":"ec263ddac77929dcfe4ba772dbf105cdac1b79653bcf95218a4106c06e93b9bc","prompt":"Write the functional spec for the remake run of \"guessling\", a command-line character-encoding detector (clean mode, stack web/Node).\n\n- App tree: guessling\n- Room: guessling.remake-room\n- Plugin root: <plugin>\n- Inputs: <room>/inventory.json (143 items, 11 UNKNOWN) and <room>/evidence/ (docs, samples, labels.json, INDEX.md, observed/ run logs). Write spec.json (source of truth) and spec.md rendered from it into <room>/spec/. Do not read <room>/original/.\n\nConstraints the spec must make testable:\n- The clone is a Node CLI started as `node <entry> [--minimal] [-l] [-e ERA] [file ...]`, reading stdin when no file is given. Tests launch REMAKE_APP_BIN with process.execPath, so the same tests drive the original and the clone.\n- Conformance is on the printed encoding name (exact casing, including the -l rename casing quirk), \"no result\"/None output, stdin naming, exit codes and stdout/stderr routing — never the confidence number. Specify confidence only as \"present\", not its value.\n- Era filtering (-e / --encoding-era), the 200,000-byte read limit, BOM handling and the observed quirks (stray ESC -> utf-8, `~{` -> HZ-GB-2312, etc.) are behaviour the original shows, so specify them where observed.\n- The 11 UNKNOWN encodings were never printed by the original; do not require them as outputs. Record them as open questions or `could` features, not `must`.\n- Prioritise: must = what the original demonstrably does on the evidence samples and the CLI contract; should = consistent secondary behaviour; could = documented-but-unobserved.\n- Strip expression: behaviour only, no wording copied from the docs beyond what the CLI literally prints.\n- Blocked (never consult): chardet, uchardet, charset-normalizer, cchardet, chardetng, jschardet.\n\nEnd with: total feature count, the must/should/could split, and a 5–8 line summary of what spec.md covers plus any open questions."}
{"at":"2026-10-19T21:03:23.858Z","agent":"remakecraft:spec-writer","id":"a8ffc7fdc74406818"}
{"at":"2026-10-19T21:23:11.274Z","event":"prompt","agent":"remakecraft:test-writer","by":"main","sha256":"432eb277d418e771b3765878ecff77c3d8a845fac05be75b23c1e432ad438c24","prompt":"Write the conformance tests for the remake run of \"guessling\", a Node command-line character-encoding detector. Spec v1 is approved and published.\n\n- App tree: guessling (spec at spec/spec.json and spec/spec.md; remake.json: stack web)\n- Room: guessling.remake-room — write visible tests to <room>/tests/visible/ and the sealed holdout to <room>/sealed/.\n- Plugin root: <plugin> (runner: scripts/runners/node-suite.mjs; see scripts/lib/stacks.mjs for the web stack conventions).\n\nHard requirements:\n- Every test launches the app as `spawn(process.execPath, [process.env.REMAKE_APP_BIN, ...args])`, so the same suites drive the original (a .mjs entry) and the clone. Read the binary path only from REMAKE_APP_BIN.\n- Assert the encoding name (exact string), \"no result\"/None output, stdin naming, exit codes and stdout/stderr routing. Never assert the confidence value; at most that it is present and within the range the spec states.\n- Tests are self-contained: input bytes come from fixtures you write next to the tests (from the spec's byte descriptions), or are built in the test with Buffer. Builders never see evidence or the room, so do not reference room paths from visible tests. Sealed tests may carry their own fixtures inside <room>/sealed/.\n- Be tolerant of line endings (LF vs CRLF is an open question in the spec).\n- `could` features with conditional checks (e.g. a library API) must skip cleanly when the clone does not provide them, not fail.\n- Cover every must and should feature in visible tests and give each feature sealed holdout cases too. Name or tag tests with their spec feature id so per-feature pass rates can be computed.\n- Do not read <room>/evidence/ or <room>/original/. Blocked names: chardet, uchardet, charset-normalizer, cchardet, chardetng, jschardet.\n- If you pass an era flag to the app in a test, use the long form --encoding-era (the short -e is also specified, test it via a dedicated case only).\n\nEnd with: number of tests per suite, per-feature coverage gaps if any, and anything in the spec you found untestable or ambiguous."}
{"at":"2026-10-19T21:23:11.447Z","agent":"remakecraft:test-writer","id":"a09f8c2b02610afea"}
{"at":"2026-10-19T22:00:40.725Z","event":"prompt","agent":"remakecraft:test-writer","by":"main","sha256":"a2aadad3810496b316af3709e7d822ce8a115b7c34d728be798d1426440e7b34","prompt":"Oracle check results for the guessling suites you wrote. Running them against the original (REMAKE_APP_BIN = the room's original entry, driven as a black box) gave visible 139/165 and sealed 52/72. Every test the original fails must be fixed, because the original is ground truth.\n\n- Room: guessling.remake-room\n- Full results (JUnit): <room>/oracle-visible.xml and <room>/oracle-sealed.xml — read them for every failing test and its message.\n- Spec (approved v1, do not edit): guessling/spec/spec.json\n- Runner: <plugin>/scripts/runners/node-suite.mjs. You may re-run it yourself from the room against the original:\n  node \"<plugin>/scripts/runners/node-suite.mjs\" tests/visible oracle-visible.xml --bin original/detect.mjs\n  node \"<plugin>/scripts/runners/node-suite.mjs\" sealed oracle-sealed.xml --bin original/detect.mjs\n  Run those commands alone, not chained. You may NOT open, read or grep anything under original/; executing it through the runner is the only allowed contact.\n\nHow to fix, by kind of failure:\n1. Content-dependent expectations on your own samples (e.g. is/cp1252.txt, is/latin-1.txt, es/*, pt/*, tr/*, uk/koi8-u.txt, ru/cp1251.txt, edge/utf8-one-char.txt, probe/hz.txt, probe/overlong.bin, probe/bad-trunc3.bin, NUL probes, `-` twice on stdin): your text does not reproduce the corpus file the spec describes. Change the fixture so the spec's rule holds on the original, or, if you can't get a sample that does, drop that assertion. Never weaken an assertion until it means nothing.\n2. Spec claims the original contradicts. The original printed ISO-8859-6 (LEGACY_ISO and ALL, for your Arabic sample) and ISO-8859-8 (LEGACY_ISO, DOS and MAINFRAME, for bg/mac-cyrillic.txt), and -l renamed them to Windows-1256 and Windows-1255. The -l casing tests on Turkish also disagree with the spec's WINDOWS-/Windows- split. Spec v1 is approved and can't change without a new human checkpoint, so: remove or narrow the assertions that depend on the contradicted claims (\"never printed\" for ISO-8859-6/8, the exact rename map, the casing split where it fails) so the suites stay true to both the original and spec v1. List each one precisely in your report; it becomes a spec v2 proposal.\n3. tty notice tests: the original's notice text contains a withheld program name. Assert what the spec states (which stream the notice goes to, that it appears, and that it mentions the end-of-input key and --help) without requiring the withheld name. Do not put the withheld name in any test.\n4. Library tests: the original entry exposes no library, and skips count as failures. Remove the library tests from both suites (the `could` library feature stays untested) and update tests/manifest.json to match.\n\nKeep feature-id naming and the sealed share near 25-33% per feature. Finish only when both suites pass 100% against the original. Report: the final counts per suite, which tests you changed, dropped or added and why, and the exact list of spec v2 proposals from point 2."}
