{"at":"2026-10-20T07:26:14.434Z","event":"prompt","agent":"remakecraft:builder","by":"main","sha256":"c548115deffd607de3eb861e7d9ba89285764ca81f487013f3d670f5b8e5199b","prompt":"Guessling clone (a Node CLI character-encoding detector), phase 5 batch 3. Your feature: `detect.era-modern-web`, and with it the single-byte scoring engine every later era feature reuses (LEGACY_ISO, LEGACY_MAC, LEGACY_REGIONAL, DOS, MAINFRAME, ALL).\n\n- App tree: guessling (your own worktree from current main). Read remake.json, spec/spec.json + spec/spec.md (v2), and tests/visible/. Run the visible suite with `node scripts/run-suite.mjs tests/visible --bin bin/guessling.mjs` and save the passing list to `.scratch/` BEFORE changing anything; use the suite's report-file argument and the Write tool if shell redirects are refused.\n- Plugin root: <plugin>.\n- Pipeline: src/detect/index.mjs runs ordered stages (BOM → binary → wide → escape → ascii → scoring). Scorers register in `SCORERS` as `{ eras, run }`, and run(window, ctx) returns an array of every candidate above its floor, `{ name, confidence, eras? }`. Era filtering, then highest confidence, picks the winner, and postSelect applies -l. src/detect/utf8.mjs is a scorer already. Its confidence for one multibyte character inside long ASCII is about 0.001, so your scorer must win those cases (spec v2: WINDOWS-1252). A short line with one character scores 0.505 and must stay utf-8. The stopgap src/detect/koi8r.mjs should be replaced by your engine.\n- Build a generic, data-driven single-byte engine:\n  - Decode candidate bytes with a byte→Unicode table per code page. Node's TextDecoder covers windows-125x, iso-8859-x, koi8-r/u, macintosh, x-mac-cyrillic and ibm866. Write tables yourself for pages it lacks (DOS CP4xx/8xx, EBCDIC, Mac variants), from the published code-page charts.\n  - Score the decoded text with per-language letter and letter-pair statistics: illegal or undefined bytes are fatal, control characters are penalised, and letter plausibility per language is scored.\n  - Write the language statistics from your own general knowledge of each language's letter frequencies. Do NOT build them from the test fixtures in tests/visible (that would overfit the sealed holdout), and consult no third-party detector code or data.\n  - Encode which code pages belong to which era in one table, taken from the spec's per-era name lists, so later builders only add pages and languages.\n- Implement MODERN_WEB fully: the Windows pages and the other single-byte names the spec lists for MODERN_WEB, with the exact printed names and casing.\n- Don't key on specific inputs. Where the spec marks a row [text-specific], don't chase it.\n- In parallel others are building detect.cjk (multibyte scorers) and detect.escape (the escape stage). Stay in your own modules (e.g. src/detect/sbcs/) plus a minimal registration edit in index.mjs.\n- Scratch files go in `.scratch/` (git-ignored), never in `.remake/`.\n- Done: the detect.era-modern-web visible tests pass, and tests in other features that need only MODERN_WEB single-byte results (e.g. detect.utf8.one_char_after_long_ascii, detect.empty-binary.overlong_utf8, output.result-line.line_with_confidence, the cp1251 cases) pass where feasible; nothing that passed before fails (diff the lists). Commit on your branch. Report before/after counts, the engine design in 5 lines, how later builders add an era tier, and the blocked tests."}
