{"at":"2026-10-20T07:26:23.716Z","event":"prompt","agent":"remakecraft:builder","by":"main","sha256":"5670ebfeed88a5b4a7a48d0131166e3e43b4b18c0dcc0e8962172dc8f3ac9f4d","prompt":"Guessling clone (a Node CLI character-encoding detector), phase 5 batch 3. Your feature: `detect.cjk`, the multibyte CJK encodings (Shift_JIS, EUC-JP, GB2312/GB18030, Big5, EUC-KR, Johab and whatever else the spec lists, with exact printed names and per-era availability).\n\n- App tree: guessling (your own worktree from current main). Read remake.json, spec/spec.json + spec/spec.md (v2), and tests/visible/. Run the visible suite with `node scripts/run-suite.mjs tests/visible --bin bin/guessling.mjs` and save the passing list to `.scratch/` BEFORE changing anything; use the suite's report-file argument and the Write tool if shell redirects are refused.\n- Plugin root: <plugin>.\n- Pipeline: src/detect/index.mjs runs ordered stages. Scorers register in `SCORERS` as `{ eras, run }`, and run(window, ctx) returns an array of every candidate above its floor, `{ name, confidence, eras? }`. Era filtering, then highest confidence, picks the winner. src/detect/utf8.mjs is a scorer already, and in parallel another builder is writing the single-byte engine. Your scorers must be confident on real CJK text and give no candidate (or a very low one) on text that isn't CJK, so they don't steal single-byte or UTF-8 results.\n- Approach: byte-structure validity per encoding (lead and trail ranges; invalid sequences are fatal), plus a distribution check of how often common characters appear. Write the common-character sets from your own general knowledge of the languages, or derive them from the code-page structure with Node's TextDecoder (it supports shift_jis, euc-jp, gbk, gb18030, big5 and euc-kr). Do NOT build statistics from the test fixtures in tests/visible, and consult no third-party detector code or data.\n- Spec rules to honour: the legacy eras give None for CJK, Johab is only in LEGACY_ISO and ALL, a