{"at":"2026-10-20T08:13:32.503Z","event":"prompt","agent":"remakecraft:builder","by":"main","sha256":"f388298aa4051c12138a1c713f6a42bb4e7cd1a1f2714e5e4d45b90d4be455e9","prompt":"Guessling clone (a Node CLI character-encoding detector): fix four reviewer findings. The visible suite is at 173/173 and must stay there.\n\n- App tree: guessling (your own worktree from current main). Read spec/spec.json + spec/spec.md (v2) as needed.\n- First save the passing list: run `node scripts/run-suite.mjs tests/visible --bin bin/guessling.mjs`. Scratch goes in `.scratch/` (git-ignored), never in `.remake/`. Use the Write tool if redirects are refused.\n\n1. **M3 (must-fix), src/detect/utf8.mjs:** the UTF-8 confidence curve `1 - 0.99 * 0.5 ** m` (capped at 0.99 from about 6 multibyte characters) reproduces another detector's formula. The spec gives no confidence values. Replace it with a curve of your own design that keeps the same qualitative behaviour:\n   - near-zero confidence for one multibyte char inside long ASCII, so a single-byte page wins;\n   - about 0.5 for one char in a short line, so it stays utf-8;\n   - rising to high confidence with more multibyte characters, weighted by density.\n   Design it from first principles, e.g. a logistic in multibyte count and density. Then re-check every switch point the tests exercise.\n2. **M5 (must-fix), src/detect/sbcs/engine.mjs:** pages that decode a text identically tie, and list order or a hair of prior then picks the winner. Example: a German line with ä ö ü ß in cp1252 can give WINDOWS-1250. Spec rule B4: a Western-language text whose characters are identical across pages goes to the Western page.\n   - Give each language a preferred page (its \"home\" page per era: German → WINDOWS-1252, Czech → WINDOWS-1250, and so on). Where readings tie within an epsilon, rank them on the uncapped score and then the language's home page.\n   - Verify that the German example (write it with the Write tool, encoded with Buffer.from(text,'latin1')) now gives WINDOWS-1252.\n3. **S6 (should-fix), src/detect/escape.mjs:** `Hello ~{word~} end` comes out as HZ-GB-2312, because lowercase ASCII byte pairs fall in GB2312's range once 0x80 is added. Require the pairs between `~{` and `~}` to decode to actual common Hanzi (or a high share of them), not just to valid code points. English text between markers must PASS and end up utf-8.\n4. **S9 (should-fix), src/detect/sbcs/engine.mjs:** English text with only a couple of curly quotes or a € (as cp1252 bytes) gives None. Per spec it should give WINDOWS-1252 in MODERN_WEB. Make punctuation-only high bytes still yield the Western page when the ASCII text around them is plausible, without letting random bytes through.\n\nDon't key on specific test inputs, and write language data from general knowledge. Done: all four fixed with probes, visible suite still 173/173 (diff the lists), committed on your branch. Report briefly."}
